        <div id="what-is-fitshow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Is FitShow?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>FitShow is two things at once: a free fitness app you install on your phone, and the
            connection platform inside the treadmill that the app talks to.</strong> Both come from the
            same company, which the Google Play listing names as FitShow (Xiamen) Information Technology
            Co., Ltd.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The app.</strong> On Google Play on 27 November 2026, FitShow was listed as
            &ldquo;FitShow: Treadmill Workout&rdquo;, an interactive indoor training app for walking,
            running, cycling and rowing that works with treadmills, exercise bikes, home trainers,
            ellipticals and <a href="/best-rowing-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>rowing machines</a>. The description says it can adjust your equipment&rsquo;s
            settings to match your training and the virtual routes you choose, and that it gives access to
            a library of geolocated videos of routes around the world. It also mentions structured training
            programs, virtual challenges and a community. The listing showed more than one million
            downloads, a first release in May 2016 and an update on 18 November 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The platform.</strong> FitShow&rsquo;s own website describes the company as an IoT
            development platform for fitness equipment, offering cloud, connectivity and an app to brands,
            retailers and manufacturers that want to make their products smart. It says the platform has
            smart-enabled more than five million pieces of equipment sold in more than 160 countries and
            regions. In plain terms: a treadmill brand can buy a console and Bluetooth module that already
            work with FitShow, rather than writing its own app. That explains why the same app turns up on
            treadmills from JAGJOG, JELENS, RUNIMON, Ksports and Aeriflo, and why it is so often a footnote
            on the listing rather than a headline.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Question</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What FitShow&rsquo;s own pages say (27 Nov 2026)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">What does it do?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Indoor training app for walking, running, cycling and rowing</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Which machines?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmills, exercise bikes, home trainers, ellipticals, rowers</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Can it change the machine&rsquo;s settings?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes, where the equipment supports it, to match your training and route</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Route videos?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A library of geolocated route videos</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cost?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free to install; Google Play lists in-app purchases of $0.99 to $59.99 per item</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Who makes it?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FitShow (Xiamen) Information Technology Co., Ltd</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            On price, the Google Play page says only that the app contains in-app purchases, priced from
            $0.99 to $59.99 per item. It does not set out which features are free and which are paid, and
            we could not load the Apple App Store page on the day we checked, so we are not quoting a
            subscription price. Open the app&rsquo;s store page on your own phone to see the current
            options before you count on any paid feature.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-controls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What “FitShow Compatible” Gets You on Each Pick</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A listing that names FitShow promises that the treadmill and the app can talk. It does
            not promise that the app can steer the machine.</strong> The app&rsquo;s description says it can
            adjust equipment settings, but that only works where the treadmill has a motor to do the
            adjusting and accepts the command. A manual-incline treadmill cannot tilt itself however good its
            Bluetooth is, and a walking pad with no incline has only speed to change.
          </p>
